WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.376
Command of Evidence: Quantitative

00:00:03.756 --> 00:00:07.752
Accurate AND relevant. Most wrong answers are only one.

00:00:08.952 --> 00:00:10.043
How to spot it

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.756
A table or graph

00:00:12.306 --> 00:00:15.543
The question says “uses data… to support”

00:00:16.093 --> 00:00:19.974
It’s testing: connecting data to one specific claim

00:00:21.174 --> 00:00:21.941
The method

00:00:22.341 --> 00:00:24.489
Step 1: Read the claim first

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.660
What comparison does it need?

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.848
Step 2: Then read the graphic

00:00:30.128 --> 00:00:31.768
Title, labels, units.

00:00:32.318 --> 00:00:34.043
Step 3: Is it accurate?

00:00:34.323 --> 00:00:35.490
Check the numbers.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.285
Step 4: Is it relevant?

00:00:38.565 --> 00:00:40.091
Does it prove THIS claim?

00:00:40.641 --> 00:00:41.735
In a few words

00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:45.000
Claim first. Accurate. Relevant.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.092
Data rules

00:00:47.492 --> 00:00:48.961
Accurate and relevant

00:00:49.241 --> 00:00:50.850
The answer must be both.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.874
Match the comparison

00:00:53.154 --> 00:00:55.063
The right groups AND the right years.

00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:56.805
Check the units

00:00:57.085 --> 00:01:00.753
Percent vs number. Thousands vs millions.

00:01:01.303 --> 00:01:03.054
A change needs two points

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:05.091
One year can’t show a trend.

00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:07.352
Let’s try one

00:01:07.752 --> 00:01:11.528
A student is researching how city residents get to work.

00:01:11.528 --> 00:01:15.917
She claims that in Portland, the share of commuters who bicycle more than doubled between 2000 and

00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:21.822
2015, while the share who walked remained nearly unchanged.

00:01:22.372 --> 00:01:27.441
Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the student’s claim?

00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:33.457
The claim has two parts: biking more than doubled, and walking stayed nearly the same.

00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:41.913
The table: biking went from 1.8 to 7.0. Walking went from 5.2 to 5.6.

00:01:42.463 --> 00:01:48.493
A, C, and D are accurate, but each one misses part of the claim. Only B shows both.

00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:50.762
Common traps

00:01:51.162 --> 00:01:52.681
Accurate but irrelevant

00:01:52.961 --> 00:01:55.514
True about the graph. Wrong claim.

00:01:56.064 --> 00:01:57.091
Half the claim

00:01:57.371 --> 00:01:58.981
Shows one trend of two.

00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:02.957
Command of Evidence: Quantitative

00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:06.352
Claim first. Accurate. Relevant.

00:02:06.902 --> 00:02:07.482
Spot it

00:02:07.682 --> 00:02:11.061
Table or graph + “uses data to support”

00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:12.318
Steps

00:02:12.518 --> 00:02:14.152
1. Read the claim first

00:02:14.432 --> 00:02:16.731
2. Read the title, labels, units

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:18.037
3. Accurate?

00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:20.094
4. Relevant to THIS claim?

00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:21.368
Remember

00:02:21.568 --> 00:02:23.038
Accurate AND relevant

00:02:23.318 --> 00:02:25.101
Right groups + right years

00:02:25.381 --> 00:02:27.697
Check units (% vs number)

00:02:27.977 --> 00:02:29.728
A change needs two points

00:02:30.278 --> 00:02:30.719
Trap

00:02:30.919 --> 00:02:32.970
True about the graph, wrong claim

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.138
Pause the video now, and copy this page into your notes.
